    "https://www.randolphcollege.edu/humanresources/faculty-searches/visiting-assistant-professor-of-art-studio-art/": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Art-Studio Art",
      "description": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Art-Studio Art - Faculty Searches - Human Resources - Randolph College Randolph College Home Page Give Today! Support Randolph College APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS A-Z ALUMNAE & ALUMNI PARENTS & FAMILIES INSIDE RANDOLPH Search the Randolph Web Site About Admission Undergraduate Graduate Academics Undergraduate Graduate Student Life Athletics Outcomes Alumnae & Alumni Parents & Families Inside Randolph APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS EVENTS Support Randolph Search ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES Human Resources Home Job Openings Faculty Searches Staff Non-Discrimination and Equal Employment Opportunity Resources for Faculty and Staff Benefits Payroll Dates Holidays Wellness Employee Handbook New Employee Information Organizational Chart Forms & Policies Student Employment Student Job Listings Links Princeton Review Top 25 Green Colleges Randolph College is ranked among the Top 40 Green Colleges by The Princeton Review October 9, 2026 Visiting Assistant Professor of Art-Studio Art Salary Range – $60,000-$70,000 Visiting Assistant Professor of Art – Studio Art The Department of Art and Art History at Randolph College invites applications for a three-year visiting position at the rank of Visiting Assistant Professor beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year.  We seek candidates with expertise in studio art and whose work is grounded in studio practice, scholarship and teaching at the collegiate level. The successful candidate will demonstrate their experience in teaching the core aspects of studio art that we offer: drawing, painting, and printmaking. Additional teaching experience and/or expertise in photography—digital and/or darkroom—and graphic design will be given additional consideration in the evaluation of your application. The cover letter should address your plans for helping students develop sustainable creative processes, including a personalized sense of connoisseurship that will enable them to thoughtfully sustain and advance their art practice beyond the classroom. We seek candidates with an active studio practice; who possess a capacity to interweave theory and practice; who demonstrate effective collaborative and interdisciplinary skills; and who display a commitment to undergraduate education in the liberal arts. Candidates must hold an MFA by July 1, 2027, a record of effective teaching in higher education, and a demonstrated record of scholarly and creative achievement. The teaching load for this position is three courses in the Fall and three courses in the Spring, with additional responsibility for advising. Candidates should submit a cover letter stating their motivation for teaching at Randolph as well as their vision for how studio art can help to enhance the history and mission of the College, a curriculum vita, a PDF with descriptions and images of up to 15 of the candidate’s works, a PDF with links and descriptions to up to 15 works by former students, and the names and contact information for three references who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation upon request. Letters will only be requested of finalists. All application materials should be uploaded via the following link: Submit Here Review of applications will begin on November 8, 2026 and the position will remain open until filled. Randolph College, founded in 1891 as Randolph-Macon Woman’s College, is a traditional small liberal arts college with a longstanding reputation for excellence in teaching and an appreciation for and commitment to inclusivity. In the College’s unique TAKE2 curricular model, students take two courses in seven-week sessions with two sessions per semester. The result is a curriculum that provides time for students to focus on two courses, while balancing other important aspects of the college experience: internships, extracurricular activities, athletics, and jobs. Lynchburg, VA is located near the geographic center of Virginia, at the edge of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Its expanding population supports a vibrant downtown, and offers a variety of cultural, educational, and recreational opportunities, making it an attractive destination for both residents and visitors. The city boasts ample outdoor activities such as hiking, swimming, boating, skiing, and access to a municipal park system with urban trails, playgrounds, and swimming pools. Lynchburg is also known for its strong educational presence, housing five colleges and universities. The city’s arts and theater scene is lively, complemented by museums and a minor league baseball team. Lynchburg is a city with a growing number of employers and businesses, but a low cost of living compared to the nearby cities of Charlottesville and Roanoke. Located approximately 180 miles from Washington, D.C., Lynchburg is accessible via an Amtrak station and regional airport. << previous post next post >> Randolph College is a private liberal arts college in Lynchburg, Virginia attracting students from Virginia, Maryland, North Carolina, and beyond. We provide a rigorous academic program that features small classes with top-ranked professors and hands-on, experiential learning. Employment Application Download PDF View Current Job Openings 2500 RIVERMONT AVE. LYNCHBURG, VA 24503 434-947-8000 CONTACT US RESOURCES FAST FACTS EMERGENCY INFO DISCLOSURE PRIVACY POLICY TITLE IX ON CAMPUS CALENDAR MAPS & DIRECTIONS CAMPUS STORE MAIER MUSEUM OF ART ENDSTATION THEATRE POPULAR LINKS A-Z INDEX PRESIDENT FINANCIAL AID EMPLOYMENT GRADUATE DEGREES FOLLOW US REQUEST ADMISSION INFO SCHEDULE A VISIT APPLY NOW GIVE TODAY"
    },
    "https://www.randolphcollege.edu/humanresources/faculty-searches/4077/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology (Anatomy and Physiology)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Biology (Anatomy and Physiology) - Faculty Searches - Human Resources - Randolph College Randolph College Home Page Give Today! Support Randolph College APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS A-Z ALUMNAE & ALUMNI PARENTS & FAMILIES INSIDE RANDOLPH Search the Randolph Web Site About Admission Undergraduate Graduate Academics Undergraduate Graduate Student Life Athletics Outcomes Alumnae & Alumni Parents & Families Inside Randolph APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS EVENTS Support Randolph Search ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES Human Resources Home Job Openings Faculty Searches Staff Non-Discrimination and Equal Employment Opportunity Resources for Faculty and Staff Benefits Payroll Dates Holidays Wellness Employee Handbook New Employee Information Organizational Chart Forms & Policies Student Employment Student Job Listings Links Best Liberal Arts Colleges in the South Randolph College was names \"One of the Best and Most Interesting Colleges in the nation\" by Forbes . September 30, 2026 Assistant Professor of Biology (Anatomy and Physiology) Salary Range $60,000 – $70,000 Assistant Professor of Biology (Anatomy and Physiology) The Department of Biological Sciences at Randolph College seeks applicants for a tenure-track assistant professor in anatomy and/or physiology to begin in August of 2027. The position requires experience in and commitment to teaching, research, and service in a small-liberal arts setting. The position is an academic (9-month) tenure-track appointment with a teaching load of six courses per year. The successful candidate will teach courses in Anatomy & Physiology, introductory biology, biology senior program, and develop new courses in the candidate’s area of expertise to complement existing course offerings. We welcome applicants from a variety of subfield backgrounds related to vertebrate anatomy and physiology but would be especially enthusiastic about candidates with teaching and research experience in embryology/developmental biology, animal physiology, animal nutrition, and/or mammalogy. The successful candidate will be expected to develop an active and independent research program focusing on any level of biological organization in animal physiology, commensurate with the needs and resources of the department. Service to the department and the institution, including undergraduate academic advising and committee work, is also required. Review of applicants will begin on November 2, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Candidates chosen to be interviewed will be contacted by email. More information about the Department of Biological Sciences at Randolph College can be found here: https://www.randolphcollege.edu/biology/ Primary responsibilities Teach six courses per academic year, including Anatomy and Physiology. Additional courses may include Introductory Biology, Senior Program, and lectures and/or laboratory courses in the candidate’s area of expertise. Typical teaching load is 3 courses per semester in our innovative Take2 curricular model where courses are offered in two 7-week sessions each semester. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Serve as an academic advisor to assigned undergraduate students (majors and non-majors). Develop and maintain an active research program that includes undergraduate students and is commensurate with the needs and resources of the department. Randolph College has established undergraduate research funding such as the Randolph Innovative Student Experience (RISE) grant, the Randolph Summer Research Program (SRP) to support summer student research, and the annual Randolph Symposium of Artists and Scholars. There are also funds available to support faculty and student travel to conferences. Start-up funds are available for this position. Serve on college-wide committees focused on faculty governance, curricular development, and enhancing campus-wide excellence. Participate in faculty meetings focused on departmental operations and planning, faculty development, pedagogical strategies, and student support initiatives. Required qualifications for tenure-track assistant professor A Ph.D. in biology, physiology, cell biology, or a closely related field by August 2027. Demonstrated or evidence of potential excellence in undergraduate instruction (in-person). A commitment to inclusive learning, active teaching strategies, and student-centered pedagogy, as demonstrated by experience, expertise, and/or formal training. Evidence of active research and scholarship. A commitment to inclusive undergraduate teaching in a liberal arts setting and a willingness to engage with institutional student success initiatives. Must be available and willing to teach in-person, on-campus courses held in a 7-week format. Preferred education and experience Teaching experience at the undergraduate level, including Human Anatomy and Physiology lecture and laboratory. Research experience that includes mentoring undergraduate students from diverse backgrounds. Experience creating a student-centered learning environment, including evidence of teaching effectiveness and/or training in pedagogical strategies. Record of publication in peer-reviewed journals, including first-authored papers. Record of acquiring extramural research funds to support the candidate’s research. How to apply To apply, please send the following materials to: biologysearch@randolphcollege.edu a cover letter addressing all position responsibilities and qualifications, including familiarity with the small liberal arts collect environment; a current curriculum vitae; a statement of teaching philosophy that includes evidence of teaching effectiveness, describes experience creating and maintaining an inclusive learning environment, and addresses ability to teach content areas in the accelerated TAKE2 learning format. an unofficial graduate transcript (official transcript required for employment); and names and contact information of three professional references (only references of finalists will be contacted). Only complete applications submitted electronically will be considered. Finalists will be required to submit to a background investigation and provide official graduate transcripts. Review of applicants will begin on November 6, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Candidates chosen to be interviewed will be contacted by email. Questions about this position may be directed to Dr. Amanda Rumore ( arumore@randolphcollege.edu ). Affirmative Action/EEO statement Randolph College is an equal opportunity employer. We do not discriminate or allow discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, gender, sexual orientation, sex (including pregnancy), gender identity, gender expression, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, marital status, veteran status, political beliefs, or any other classification protected by applicable law. This policy applies to all terms and conditions of employment, including but not limited to hiring, promotion, termination, placement, transfer, layoff, recall, compensation, and training. About the Department of Biological Sciences at Randolph College Our department focuses on integrative biology and emphasizes evolution across our curriculum. The department consists of six faculty and a full-time laboratory technician. Members of the department have access to teaching and research lab space in the newly-renovated Martin Science Building, the Randolph College Natural History and Archaeology Collections, and multiple college-owned nature preserves. More information about our department can be found here:  http://www.randolphcollege.edu/biology/ About Randolph College Randolph College, founded in 1891 as Randolph-Macon Woman’s College, is a traditional small liberal arts college with a longstanding reputation for excellence in teaching and an appreciation for and commitment to inclusivity. In the College’s unique TAKE2 curricular model, students take two courses in seven-week sessions with two sessions per semester. The result is a curriculum that provides time for students to focus on two courses, while balancing other important aspects of the college experience: internships, extracurricular activities, athletics, and jobs. More information about Randolph College can be found here: http://www.randolphcollege.edu/ About Lynchburg, VA Lynchburg, VA is located near the geographic center of Virginia, at the edge of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Its expanding population supports a vibrant downtown, and offers a variety of cultural, educational, and recreational opportunities, making it an attractive destination for both residents and visitors. The city boasts ample outdoor activities such as hiking, swimming, boating, skiing, and access to a municipal park system with urban trails, playgrounds, and swimming pools. Lynchburg is also known for its strong educational presence, housing five colleges and universities. The city’s arts and theater scene is lively, complemented by museums and a minor league baseball team. Lynchburg is a city with a growing number of employers and businesses, but a low cost of living compared to the nearby cities of Charlottesville and Roanoke. Located approximately 180 miles from Washington, D.C., Lynchburg is accessible via an Amtrak station and regional airport. << previous post next post >> Randolph College is a private liberal arts college in Lynchburg, Virginia attracting students from Virginia, Maryland, North Carolina, and beyond. We provide a rigorous academic program that features small classes with top-ranked professors and hands-on, experiential learning. Employment Application Download PDF View Current Job Openings 2500 RIVERMONT AVE. LYNCHBURG, VA 24503 434-947-8000 CONTACT US RESOURCES FAST FACTS EMERGENCY INFO DISCLOSURE PRIVACY POLICY TITLE IX ON CAMPUS CALENDAR MAPS & DIRECTIONS CAMPUS STORE MAIER MUSEUM OF ART ENDSTATION THEATRE POPULAR LINKS A-Z INDEX PRESIDENT FINANCIAL AID EMPLOYMENT GRADUATE DEGREES FOLLOW US REQUEST ADMISSION INFO SCHEDULE A VISIT APPLY NOW GIVE TODAY"
    },
    "https://www.randolphcollege.edu/humanresources/faculty-searches/assistant-professor-of-education-generalist/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Education (Generalist)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Education (Generalist) - Faculty Searches - Human Resources - Randolph College Randolph College Home Page Give Today! Support Randolph College APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS A-Z ALUMNAE & ALUMNI PARENTS & FAMILIES INSIDE RANDOLPH Search the Randolph Web Site About Admission Undergraduate Graduate Academics Undergraduate Graduate Student Life Athletics Outcomes Alumnae & Alumni Parents & Families Inside Randolph APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS EVENTS Support Randolph Search ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES Human Resources Home Job Openings Faculty Searches Staff Non-Discrimination and Equal Employment Opportunity Resources for Faculty and Staff Benefits Payroll Dates Holidays Wellness Employee Handbook New Employee Information Organizational Chart Forms & Policies Student Employment Student Job Listings Links Princeton Review Most Accessible Professors Randolph College was ranked 23rd in the nation by The Princeton Review for the \"Most Accessible Professors.\" September 22, 2026 Assistant Professor of Education (Generalist) Salary Range – $60,000 – $70,000 Assistant Professor of Education The Education Department at Randolph College seeks a dynamic tenure-track assistant professor of education to begin July 1, 2027: We seek one faculty member with expertise in curriculum and instruction at the elementary or secondary level , with preference for elementary and/or secondary reading instruction. We welcome applications from candidates who are members of historically underrepresented groups. We expect our new colleague to be committed to inclusive, equitable teaching. This faculty member will likely lead student teaching seminars and may also teach other undergraduate and graduate courses in evidence-based reading instruction, reading and writing in the content areas, instructional methods, or foundations of education. Primary responsibilities Teach a 3/3 load with a mix of online and face-to-face classes. Advise undergraduate students. Supervise undergraduate and graduate preservice teachers in local and distance practicum experiences, depending on course assignment. Maintain an active scholarly agenda resulting in presentations at local, regional, and national conferences and publications. Maintain active membership in relevant state and national organizations. Support the Randolph College Educator Preparation Program’s accreditation and program approval processes. Collaborate with local schools and school leaders to create and sustain mutually beneficial partnerships. Engage in service to the College, community, and profession. Demonstrate a strong understanding of and commitment to a liberal arts education. Demonstrate a strong understanding of the social constructivist approach to education and commitment to preparing teachers able to combine research skills, social responsibility, effective instructional practice, content expertise, and leadership skills in their future classrooms. Required qualifications A PhD or EdD in curriculum and instruction or a related field. (ABD candidates with a plan to finish by August 2027 will be considered.) At least three years of experience as a licensed teacher in grades PK–12 in a public U.S. school. Evidence of or potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate instruction. Evidence of an active or burgeoning research agenda. The Randolph College Education Department prepares undergraduate and graduate students for licensure in elementary education, special education, and a number of secondary/all-grades areas. At the undergraduate level, we support two undergraduate majors (elementary and special education); a minor in race, culture, and equity in education; and a secondary/all-grades licensure pathway. At the graduate level, we offer a fully online Master of Arts in Teaching leading to licensure and Master of Education for candidates not seeking licensure. Our department prepares candidates to teach effectively, to act as leaders in school communities, and to serve as agents of equity and change. Our work reflects the College’s motto of vita abundantior —a life more abundant—by promoting joy, curiosity, and the love of learning as essential parts of a meaningful life. The philosophical foundation of Randolph’s Educator Preparation Program begins with the fundamental belief that culture and community influence learning, and our social constructivist approach helps candidates grow as culturally relevant and responsive educators. Our primary goal is to foster the development of highly qualified teachers and leaders in PK–12 schools and a range of other educational settings. How to Apply To apply, please send the following materials to educationsearch@randolphcollege.edu : A cover letter addressing position responsibilities and qualifications; A current curriculum vitae; A statement of teaching philosophy that includes evidence of teaching effectiveness and alignment with the department’s social constructivist philosophy and commitment to inclusivity; An unofficial graduate transcript (official transcript required at time of hire); and Names and contact information for three references (letters will be requested for those candidates who are interviewed). Review of materials will begin on October 16, 2026. About Randolph College Randolph College, founded in 1891 as Randolph-Macon Woman’s College, is a traditional small liberal arts college with a longstanding reputation for excellence in teaching and an appreciation for and commitment to inclusivity. In the College’s unique TAKE2 curricular model, students take two courses in 7-week sessions with two sessions per semester. The result is a curriculum that provides time for students to focus on two courses, while balancing other important aspects of the college experience: internships, extracurricular activities, athletics, and jobs. More information about Randolph College can be found here: http://www.randolphcollege.edu/ About Lynchburg, VA Founded in 1757 by abolitionist John Lynch, Lynchburg, VA is located near the geographic center of Virginia, at the edge of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Its expanding population supports a vibrant downtown, and offers a variety of cultural, educational, and recreational opportunities, making it an attractive destination for both residents and visitors. The city boasts ample outdoor activities such as hiking, swimming, boating, skiing, and access to a municipal park system with urban trails, playgrounds, and swimming pools. The city’s arts and theater scene is lively, complemented by museums and a minor league baseball team. Lynchburg is a city with a growing number of employers and businesses, but a low cost of living compared to the nearby cities of Charlottesville and Roanoke. Located approximately 180 miles from Washington, D.C., Lynchburg is accessible via an Amtrak station and regional airport. Lynchburg is also known for its strong educational presence, housing five colleges and universities, including one HBCU. The city was the home of the Virginia Teachers’ Reading Circle. Established in 1887, the Reading Circle provided professional development for teachers of Black students across Virginia. This group evolved into the Virginia Teachers Association, serving Black teachers in the region until it merged with the integrated Virginia Education Association in 1967. This history of diversity continues to be reflected in the city’s schools today. << previous post next post >> Randolph College is a private liberal arts college in Lynchburg, Virginia attracting students from Virginia, Maryland, North Carolina, and beyond. We provide a rigorous academic program that features small classes with top-ranked professors and hands-on, experiential learning. Employment Application Download PDF View Current Job Openings 2500 RIVERMONT AVE. LYNCHBURG, VA 24503 434-947-8000 CONTACT US RESOURCES FAST FACTS EMERGENCY INFO DISCLOSURE PRIVACY POLICY TITLE IX ON CAMPUS CALENDAR MAPS & DIRECTIONS CAMPUS STORE MAIER MUSEUM OF ART ENDSTATION THEATRE POPULAR LINKS A-Z INDEX PRESIDENT FINANCIAL AID EMPLOYMENT GRADUATE DEGREES FOLLOW US REQUEST ADMISSION INFO SCHEDULE A VISIT APPLY NOW GIVE TODAY"
    },
    "https://www.randolphcollege.edu/humanresources/faculty-searches/assistant-professor-of-education-special-education/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Education (Special Education)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Education (Special Education) - Faculty Searches - Human Resources - Randolph College Randolph College Home Page Give Today! Support Randolph College APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS A-Z ALUMNAE & ALUMNI PARENTS & FAMILIES INSIDE RANDOLPH Search the Randolph Web Site About Admission Undergraduate Graduate Academics Undergraduate Graduate Student Life Athletics Outcomes Alumnae & Alumni Parents & Families Inside Randolph APPLY REQUEST VISIT NEWS EVENTS Support Randolph Search ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES ABOUT ADMISSION Undergraduate Graduate ACADEMICS Undergraduate Graduate STUDENT LIFE ATHLETICS OUTCOMES Human Resources Home Job Openings Faculty Searches Staff Non-Discrimination and Equal Employment Opportunity Resources for Faculty and Staff Benefits Payroll Dates Holidays Wellness Employee Handbook New Employee Information Organizational Chart Forms & Policies Student Employment Student Job Listings Links Princeton Review Top 25 Green Colleges Randolph College is ranked among the Top 40 Green Colleges by The Princeton Review September 22, 2026 Assistant Professor of Education (Special Education) Salary Range – $60,000-$70,000 Assistant Professor of Education The Education Department at Randolph College seeks a dynamic tenure-track assistant professor of education to begin July 1, 2027: We seek a faculty member with expertise in special education . Candidates with experience in evidence-based reading instruction are preferred. We welcome applications from candidates who are members of historically underrepresented groups. We expect our new colleague to be committed to inclusive, equitable teaching. This faculty member will likely teach Educational Psychology as well as other undergraduate and graduate courses in special education, including Instructional Approaches for Students with Disabilities and Practicum, Assessment and Evaluation in Special Education, IEP Implementation & Case Management and Transition, Survey of Special Ed and Special Ed Law, as well as graduate methods classes. The faculty member will also organize and facilitate the spring Special Education Conference for area teachers. Primary responsibilities Teach a 3/3 load with a mix of online and face-to-face classes. Advise undergraduate students. Supervise undergraduate and graduate preservice teachers in local and distance practicum experiences, depending on course assignment. Maintain an active scholarly agenda resulting in presentations at local, regional, and national conferences and publications. Maintain active membership in relevant state and national organizations. Support the Randolph College Educator Preparation Program’s accreditation and program approval processes. Collaborate with local schools and school leaders to create and sustain mutually beneficial partnerships. Engage in service to the College, community, and profession. Demonstrate a strong understanding of and commitment to a liberal arts education. Demonstrate a strong understanding of the social constructivist approach to education and commitment to preparing teachers able to combine research skills, social responsibility, effective instructional practice, content expertise, and leadership skills in their future classrooms. Required qualifications A PhD or EdD in special education, curriculum and instruction, or a related field. (ABD candidates with a plan to finish by August 2027 will be considered.) At least three years of experience as a licensed teacher in grades PK–12 in a public U.S. school. Evidence of or potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate instruction. Evidence of an active or burgeoning research agenda. The Randolph College Education Department prepares undergraduate and graduate students for licensure in elementary education, special education, and a number of secondary/all-grades areas. At the undergraduate level, we support two undergraduate majors (elementary and special education); a minor in race, culture, and equity in education; and a secondary/all-grades licensure pathway. At the graduate level, we offer a fully online Master of Arts in Teaching leading to licensure and Master of Education for candidates not seeking licensure. Our department prepares candidates to teach effectively, to act as leaders in school communities, and to serve as agents of equity and change. Our work reflects the College’s motto of vita abundantior —a life more abundant—by promoting joy, curiosity, and the love of learning as essential parts of a meaningful life. The philosophical foundation of Randolph’s Educator Preparation Program begins with the fundamental belief that culture and community influence learning, and our social constructivist approach helps candidates grow as culturally relevant and responsive educators. Our primary goal is to foster the development of highly qualified teachers and leaders in PK–12 schools and a range of other educational settings. How to Apply To apply, please send the following materials to specialedsearch@randolphcollege.edu : A cover letter addressing position responsibilities and qualifications; A current curriculum vitae; A statement of teaching philosophy that includes evidence of teaching effectiveness and alignment with the department’s social constructivist philosophy and commitment to inclusivity; An unofficial graduate transcript (official transcript required at time of hire); and Names and contact information for three references (letters will be requested for those candidates who are interviewed). Review of materials will begin on October 16, 2026. About Randolph College Randolph College, founded in 1891 as Randolph-Macon Woman’s College, is a traditional small liberal arts college with a longstanding reputation for excellence in teaching and an appreciation for and commitment to inclusivity. In the College’s unique TAKE2 curricular model, students take two courses in 7-week sessions with two sessions per semester. The result is a curriculum that provides time for students to focus on two courses, while balancing other important aspects of the college experience: internships, extracurricular activities, athletics, and jobs. More information about Randolph College can be found here: http://www.randolphcollege.edu/ About Lynchburg, VA Founded in 1757 by abolitionist John Lynch, Lynchburg, VA is located near the geographic center of Virginia, at the edge of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Its expanding population supports a vibrant downtown, and offers a variety of cultural, educational, and recreational opportunities, making it an attractive destination for both residents and visitors. The city boasts ample outdoor activities such as hiking, swimming, boating, skiing, and access to a municipal park system with urban trails, playgrounds, and swimming pools. The city’s arts and theater scene is lively, complemented by museums and a minor league baseball team. Lynchburg is a city with a growing number of employers and businesses, but a low cost of living compared to the nearby cities of Charlottesville and Roanoke. Located approximately 180 miles from Washington, D.C., Lynchburg is accessible via an Amtrak station and regional airport. Lynchburg is also known for its strong educational presence, housing five colleges and universities, including one HBCU. The city was the home of the Virginia Teachers’ Reading Circle. Established in 1887, the Reading Circle provided professional development for teachers of Black students across Virginia. This group evolved into the Virginia Teachers Association, serving Black teachers in the region until it merged with the integrated Virginia Education Association in 1967. This history of diversity continues to be reflected in the city’s schools today. << previous post next post >> Randolph College is a private liberal arts college in Lynchburg, Virginia attracting students from Virginia, Maryland, North Carolina, and beyond. We provide a rigorous academic program that features small classes with top-ranked professors and hands-on, experiential learning. Employment Application Download PDF View Current Job Openings 2500 RIVERMONT AVE. LYNCHBURG, VA 24503 434-947-8000 CONTACT US RESOURCES FAST FACTS EMERGENCY INFO DISCLOSURE PRIVACY POLICY TITLE IX ON CAMPUS CALENDAR MAPS & DIRECTIONS CAMPUS STORE MAIER MUSEUM OF ART ENDSTATION THEATRE POPULAR LINKS A-Z INDEX PRESIDENT FINANCIAL AID EMPLOYMENT GRADUATE DEGREES FOLLOW US REQUEST ADMISSION INFO SCHEDULE A VISIT APPLY NOW GIVE TODAY"
